.Dd November 14, 2026
.Dt PIDFD_OPEN 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pidfd_open ,
.Nm pidfd_send_signal ,
.Nm pidfd_getfd
.Nd descriptors that name a process
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/pidfd.h
.Ft int
.Fn pidfd_open "pid_t pid" "unsigned int flags"
.Ft int
.Fn pidfd_send_signal "int pidfd" "int sig" "siginfo_t *info" "unsigned int flags"
.Ft int
.Fn pidfd_getfd "int pidfd" "int targetfd" "unsigned int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A pidfd is a descriptor that names a process.
Unlike a process ID, it never comes to name another process: once its own
has been reaped and its number given to a new one, the pidfd names none.
.Pp
.Fn pidfd_open
returns a new close-on-exec pidfd of the process
.Fa pid
leads, which may have ended and not yet been reaped.
.Fa flags
is 0 or
.Dv PIDFD_NONBLOCK ,
which makes the descriptor non-blocking.
.Xr fork 2 Ns 's
.Dv CLONE_PIDFD
makes one of the child as it is created.
.Pp
.Xr poll 2
and
.Xr epoll 2
report a pidfd readable once every thread of its process has ended, and
hung up as well once the process has been reaped.
Both events wake a poller already waiting on the pidfd.
.Xr waitid 2
with
.Dv P_PIDFD
waits for the process, when it is a child of the caller's.
A
.Xr read 2
or
.Xr write 2
of a pidfd fails with
.Er EINVAL .
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /fd
names it
.Dq anon_inode:[pidfd] .
.Pp
.Fn pidfd_send_signal
sends
.Fa sig
under the permission of
.Xr kill 2 ,
where
.Fa flags
says, which is 0 or one of:
.Bl -tag -width "PIDFD_SIGNAL_PROCESS_GROUP"
.It Dv PIDFD_SIGNAL_THREAD
the thread that leads the process alone, as
.Xr tgkill 2
sends it;
.It Dv PIDFD_SIGNAL_THREAD_GROUP
the process, as
.Xr kill 2
sends it to a process ID, which 0 also does;
.It Dv PIDFD_SIGNAL_PROCESS_GROUP
each member of the process group whose ID is the process's, as
.Xr kill 2
sends it to the negated ID, even once the process has been reaped.
.El
.Pp
A null
.Fa info
gives the receiver
.Xr kill 2 Ns 's
siginfo,
.Dv SI_TKILL Ns 's
for a thread; otherwise
.Fa info
is the siginfo, whose
.Va si_signo
must be
.Fa sig ,
and whose code a thread may give a process it does not lead, or any
process group, only when it is negative and not
.Dv SI_TKILL ,
as
.Xr rt_sigqueueinfo 2
has it; a native caller's siginfo is read and refused as there when its
code's layout is unknown and it has bytes past the kept fields.
Signal 0 checks the permission and sends nothing, and a signal to a process
whose threads have all ended, or to a leading thread that has ended, is
discarded.
The checks come in Linux's order:
.Fa flags ,
then the pidfd and the process's PID namespace, which the pidfd keeps
after the reap, then the copy of
.Fa info ,
its
.Va si_signo
and its code, then whether the process has been reaped or the group is
empty, and only then
.Fa sig
and the permission.
The kernel holds the pidfd's process across the copy and finds it again
after, so a page fault during the copy cannot leave a stale target.
.Pp
.Fn pidfd_getfd
returns a close-on-exec copy, in the caller's table, of the process's
descriptor
.Fa targetfd ,
which shares its open file description, as
.Xr dup 2
makes a copy.
The process must be the caller's own, or the caller must be one that may
attach to it with
.Xr ptrace 2 :
privileged, or with a real user ID and a real group ID equal to each of
the process's three, while the process is dumpable and not privileged.
.Fa flags
must be 0.
.Ss System call interface
The calls are numbered the same on i386 and x86-64:
.Nm pidfd_send_signal
424,
.Nm pidfd_open
434 and
.Nm pidfd_getfd
438.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn pidfd_open
and
.Fn pidfd_getfd
return the new descriptor and
.Fn pidfd_send_signal
returns 0; each returns -1 with
.Va errno
set on failure.
The kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/pidfdmusl.c
opens, polls, signals and waits on pidfds, takes a pipe through
.Fn pidfd_getfd ,
and checks the refusals.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EMFILE]"
.It Bq Er E2BIG
.Fa info
has a code of a layout Linux does not know and a byte past the kept
fields that is not zero, as
.Xr rt_sigqueueinfo 2
describes.
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa pidfd
is not an open pidfd, or
.Fa targetfd
is not open in the process, which has none once it has ended.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa info
cannot be read.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa pid
is not above 0 or names a thread that does not lead its process;
.Fa flags
has a bit not described above, or more than one of
.Fn pidfd_send_signal Ns 's;
.Fa sig
is out of range for a process not reaped, or is not the
.Va si_signo
of
.Fa info ;
or the pidfd's process, reaped or not, is outside the caller's PID
namespace and those below it.
.It Bq Er EMFILE
The caller has no free descriptor below its limit.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The kernel had no memory for the pidfd, a larger descriptor table or an
open file description.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller may not signal the process or any member of the group, give it
that siginfo code, or take its descriptors.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
No process has the ID
.Fa pid ,
the pidfd's process has been reaped, or the process group
.Dv PIDFD_SIGNAL_PROCESS_GROUP
names has no members.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr kill 2 ,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr tgkill 2 ,
.Xr wait 2
